          "line": 10,
          "text": "  - def-it-point-to-point-joint-source-channel-code"
        },
        {
          "line": 11,
          "text": "  - lem-it-finite-iid-source-lossless-code-achievability"
        },
        {
          "line": 41,
          "text": "Let $S_1,S_2,\\ldots$ be IID with probability vector $P_S$ on a finite nonempty alphabet $\\mathcal S$, and let $W$ be a finite DMC with capacity $C(W)$ in nats per channel use. Write $H(P_S):=-\\sum_{s:P_S(s)>0}P_S(s)\\ln P_S(s)$ for source entropy in nats. Fix positive integers $n_k$ with $n_k/k\\to\\rho\\in(0,\\infty)$. A deterministic $(k,n_k)$ joint code is as in [[def-it-point-to-point-joint-source-channel-code]]. If $H(P_S)=0$, a zero-error code sequence exists for every such $W$. If $H(P_S)<\\rho C(W)$, there are codes with block error tending to zero. Conversely, every code sequence with block error tending to zero satisfies $H(P_S)\\le\\rho C(W)$. Thus strict violation $H(P_S)>\\rho C(W)$ rules out reliable transmission; the positive-entropy equality boundary is not asserted."
        },
        {
          "line": 49,
          "text": "[F1] [[def-it-point-to-point-joint-source-channel-code]]: an encoder maps $\\mathcal S^k\\to\\mathcal X^{n_k}$ and a decoder maps $\\mathcal Y^{n_k}\\to\\mathcal S^k$ in the lossless case."
        },
        {
          "line": 55,
          "text": "[F4] [[lem-it-finite-iid-source-lossless-code-achievability]]: every source-code rate strictly above $H(P_S)$ admits deterministic fixed-length source codes with block error tending to zero."
        }
      ]
    },
